  <!-- Headline -->
  <h2>What Actually Makes BNEsim Different</h2>
  <p>
    BNEsim doesn't sell a flat global data-only SIM the way some competitors do. It sells per-country and per-region data packs, and each one comes with a choice: a cheaper pack that expires in 30 days, or a pricier version of the same pack with no expiration at all. That second option matters if you travel somewhere occasionally rather than on a fixed schedule; you can buy data for a country now and it sits ready whenever you actually land there, instead of running out the clock while you're not using it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The site also runs on a credit-based account rather than a straight one-off checkout: you top up a balance and draw from it across purchases, and it accepts a wider range of payment methods than most providers on this site, including crypto. You can also schedule a plan to start activating on a future date instead of the moment you buy it, which is a small but genuinely useful detail if you're buying ahead of a trip and don't want the clock to start early.
  </p>

  <!-- Real operational note instead of generic feature grid / comparison table -->
  <h2>The Discounts Are Already in the Price, Not a Code</h2>
  <p>
    BNEsim's own pricing pages show discounts of up to 70% as a struck-through original price next to a lower sale price, applied automatically rather than through anything you type in at checkout. That's a real difference from most providers on this site, where the sticker price is the sticker price and a code is the only way to bring it down. It also means a page like this one, built around a promo code, isn't really how BNEsim's pricing works in the first place; the fairest thing we can tell you is to compare the sale price shown on their site against a competitor's price plus their code, rather than assume BNEsim needs a code to be competitive.
  </p>

